\title{The Schnorr Protocol}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-21}

\p{
    Schnorr is the worked example of a [sigma protocol](dhsorens-004U): three messages in which a
    prover convinces a verifier that it knows the discrete logarithm of a group element, and reveals
    nothing else. \em{Zero-knowledge proof of knowledge} is nearly the right name for it, but
    under-specifies it twice. \em{Of knowledge} is the load-bearing half — every element of a
    [cyclic group](dhsorens-004W) generated by #{g} has a discrete logarithm, so the statement has
    no false instances and plain [soundness](dhsorens-004J) says nothing about it. And the
    zero-knowledge is \em{special honest-verifier}: the simulator below needs the challenge in
    advance and assumes it was drawn uniformly. \em{Honest-verifier zero-knowledge proof of
    knowledge of a discrete logarithm} is the accurate mouthful.
}

\p{
    The setting is a [cyclic group](dhsorens-004W) #{G} of prime order #{q} with generator #{g}, in
    which the discrete logarithm is hard. The statement is a group element #{h \in G}; the witness
    is the exponent #{w \in \mathbb{Z}_q} with #{g^w = h}.
}

\p{
    The protocol:
    \ul{
        \li{The prover draws #{r} uniformly from #{\mathbb{Z}_q} and sends the commitment
        #{a = g^r}.}
        \li{The verifier draws a challenge #{e} uniformly from #{\mathbb{Z}_q} and sends it.}
        \li{The prover sends the response #{z = r + e w \bmod q}.}
    }
    The verifier accepts exactly when
}

##{g^z = a \cdot h^e.}

\p{
    \strong{Completeness} is the exponent arithmetic. Substituting #{z = r + ew},
}

##{g^z = g^{r + e w} = g^r (g^w)^e = a \cdot h^e,}

\p{
    so an honest prover is always accepted and the completeness error is zero.
}

\p{
    \strong{Special soundness} is one subtraction. Two accepting transcripts #{(a, e, z)} and
    #{(a, e', z')} on the same commitment satisfy #{g^z = a h^e} and #{g^{z'} = a h^{e'}}. Dividing
    the first by the second cancels #{a}:
}

##{g^{z - z'} = h^{e - e'}.}

\p{
    Since #{q} is prime and #{e \neq e'}, the difference #{e - e'} is invertible in #{\mathbb{Z}_q},
    so
}

##{w = (z - z')(e - e')^{-1} \bmod q}

\p{
    is the discrete logarithm of #{h}. That is the extractor in full: subtract, invert, stop. Two
    field operations, and no assumption about the prover beyond its having produced the two
    transcripts. It is also where the prime order earns its keep — at composite order #{e - e'} may
    fail to be invertible and the step collapses.
}

\p{
    \strong{Special honest-verifier zero knowledge} is the same equation read backwards. Given #{h}
    and a challenge #{e}, the simulator draws #{z} uniformly from #{\mathbb{Z}_q} and sets
    #{a = g^z h^{-e}}, so the transcript #{(a, e, z)} satisfies the verification equation by
    construction. The distribution matches exactly, not approximately. In a real transcript #{r} is
    uniform, hence #{z = r + ew} is uniform and independent of #{e}, and #{a} is then pinned down by
    #{z} and #{e} through the verification equation. Both distributions are therefore uniform #{z}
    with #{a} determined, so they are the same distribution — and #{w} cannot be recovered from a
    simulated transcript because it never entered.
}

\p{
    Three consequences outside the definition. [Fiat–Shamir](dhsorens-002G) replaces the verifier
    with a hash, #{e = H(g, h, a)}, turning the protocol into the Schnorr signature scheme —
    deployed as Ed25519 and, since Taproot, as BIP-340 in [Bitcoin](dhsorens-bitcoin) — and the
    #{(Q+1)\kappa} bound of [special soundness](dhsorens-004K) is what makes the transformed
    protocol analysable at all. Nonce reuse is the extractor turned against its owner: two
    signatures sharing #{a} under different challenges hand over #{w} by the subtraction above,
    which is how signing keys have actually been lost. And the knowledge error #{1/q} is a claim
    about the challenge set, not the group — a prover with no witness can guess a challenge,
    simulate a transcript for it, and win with that probability, so a small challenge space has to
    be [repeated](dhsorens-004P) rather than trusted.
}

\p{
    Sources: Schnorr, \em{Efficient Signature Generation by Smart Cards} (Journal of Cryptology,
    1991); Damgård, [On Σ-protocols](https://www.cs.au.dk/~ivan/Sigma.pdf), for the extraction and
    simulation written out; [VCV-io](dhsorens-0042) formalises sigma protocols and the Fiat–Shamir
    transform in [Lean](dhsorens-lean).
}
